Z||000000||batboy||10-23-2000||08:47 PM||||The other day, I was in the city when I saw a Fiero GT behind me making lots of agressive lane changes and driving fast. I watch him in the rearview mirror cutting other drivers off because he had to be in front of the pack. He drew along side me, obviously wanting in my lane in front of me, but I wouldn't let him. He made three runs on me and I walked away each time. Finally realizing defeated, he gave up and dropped in behind me, then eventually turned off on a side street. Oh yeah, did I forget to mention I was driving my new 98 Grand Prix GT?||||reg||12
Z||000001||wkayl||10-23-2000||09:19 PM||||Bragger.  But then if I could talk my wife into getting a Grand Prix GT I would too.  My truck will have it's last payment in December and I mentioned getting a Grand Prix GT, but she said we have too many cars now (3 cars and 1 truck).  She wants to get a new pickup NEXT fall.  We now have a 90 Chev, ext cab, 4x4, short-bed with 141,000 miles.  Looking at getting a black one of the same only a 2001 or maybe 2002 by then.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-23-2000||11:35 PM||||What a doorknob (the guy in the GT, that is).  Driving fast is one thing, but making aggressive lane changes and dorking around like that in town... just so you can catch the next red light before everyone else, is plain stupid.  Last thing we need around here is another ex(wrecked)-Fiero GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <P>In his defense, though, apparently he finally saw it wasn't getting him anywhere fast, and gave it up.  I hope, anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Some people, you try to give them a hint, and they just act more stupid.        <P>   ||||reg||13
